  planner_response {
    modified_response: "To determine which screen you are actively looking at and find its corresponding source code file, we can look at how the sidebar navigation and page routing are designed in this application.\n\nHere is a straightforward guide on how to identify any screen you occupy and its source file:\n\n### 1. Identify the Screen via the Left Sidebar Selection\nThe left navigation bar (`Sidebar`) highlights the active menu item using a **blue vertical bar** and an indented highlight. \n\nThe exact mapping between the label shown in the sidebar and the React view component being displayed is defined inside `/src/components/layout/Sidebar.tsx` and routed in `/src/App.tsx`. \n\nHere is the complete reference map:\n\n| Sidebar Label | Active Tab ID | React View Component | Component Source File Path |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Dashboard** | `dashboard` | `<DashboardView />` | `/src/components/views/DashboardView.tsx` |\n| **IETF Domains** | `ietf-explorer` | `<IETFExplorerView />` | `/src/components/views/IETFExplorerView.tsx` |\n| **YANG Validator** | `yang-validator` | `<YANGValidatorView />` | `/src/components/views/YANGValidatorView.tsx` |\n| **Inventory Location** | `ni-locations` | `<NetworkLocationsView />` | `/src/components/views/NetworkLocationsView.tsx` |\n| **Network Inventory** | `network-inventory` | `<NetworkInventoryView />` | `/src/components/views/NetworkInventoryView.tsx` |\n| **Passive Inventory** | `passive-inventory` | `<PassiveNetworkInventoryView />` | `/src/components/views/PassiveNetworkInventoryView.tsx` |\n| **Base Network Topology** | `base-topology` | `<BaseNetworkTopologyView />` | `/src/components/views/BaseNetworkTopologyView.tsx` |\n| **Internet Types (RFC 6021)** | `internet-types` | `<InternetTypesView />` | `/src/components/views/InternetTypesView.tsx` |\n| **Optical L0 (RFC 9093)** | `optical-l0` | `<OpticalLayer0View />` | `/src/components/views/OpticalLayer0View.tsx` |\n| **Optical L1 (Draft-IETF)** | `optical-l1` | `<OpticalLayer1View />` | `/src/components/views/OpticalLayer1View.tsx` |\n| **Inventory Topology** | `network-inventory-topology` | `<NetworkInventoryTopologyView />` | `/src/components/views/NetworkInventoryTopologyView.tsx` |\n| **Topology** | `topology` | `<TopologyView />` | `/src/components/views/TopologyView.tsx` |\n| **Devices** | `devices` | `<DevicesView />` | `/src/components/views/DevicesView.tsx` |\n| **Links** | `links` | `<LinksView />` | `/src/components/views/LinksView.tsx` |\n| **Services** | `services` | `<ServicesView />` | `/src/components/views/ServicesView.tsx` |\n| **Slices** | `slices` | `<SlicesView />` | `/src/components/views/SlicesView.tsx` |\n\n---\n\n### 2. Identify the \"Drill-Down\" Detail Screen\nIf you are looking at a detail page of a specific device, link, service, slice, or component (usually triggered by clicking an identifier link or item), the sidebar highlighting will clear out (or show the previous main tab). \n\n* **Active View Name**: **Object Detail View**\n* **React Component**: `<DetailView />`\n* **Source File Path**: `/src/components/views/DetailView.tsx`\n\n---\n\n### How to Confirm Programmatically (Or if UI headers are disabled/empty)\nIf a view has no visible title or is visually blank, you can identify it in the code by opening `/src/App.tsx`. \n\nLook at the `renderView()` logic (around line 62). It returns the exact JSX component based on the `activeTab` string state:\n```tsx\nconst renderView = () => {\n  if (activeTab === \'detail\' && currentItem) {\n    return <DetailView item={currentItem} onNavigate={handleNavigate} onBack={handleBack} />;\n  }\n\n  switch (activeTab) {\n    case \'dashboard\': return <DashboardView onNavigate={handleNavigate} />;\n    case \'ietf-explorer\': return <IETFExplorerView onNavigate={handleNavigate} />;\n    // ...\n  }\n};\n```"
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
